---
title: Live & CMS sources
summary: Beyond files on disk — fetch from an API, or stream entries from something that pushes.
related:
  - content/collections
  - data-state/held-regions
---

A `loader` is not limited to a glob. Any object with `get` / `list` / `ids` is a source, so the same collection API covers a REST API and a source that pushes over time.

> **When to use.** Write a fetching source when your content lives in a **CMS or API** rather than files, and add `live()` when the source **pushes updates** you want to stream. Everything else — schema, relations, `list`, `get` — is identical to a file-backed [collection](/docs/content/collections).

| Source | Write |
| --- | --- |
| Markdown / JSON / YAML in repo | `mdsvex(glob(…))` · `json(glob(…))` · `blocks(glob(…), registry)` |
| A REST API | a `{ get, list, ids }` source that fetches |
| Something that **pushes** | add `live()` — a change signal — + `live.list` / `live.get` |

## Fetch from an API

A source is three async methods. `get(id)` fetches one entry, `list()` the set, `ids()` the ids:

```ts
import { content } from 'ogygia/content';

const press = content({
  schema,
  loader: {
    async get(id) {
      const row = await fetch(`https://cms/posts/${id}`).then((r) => r.json());
      return row ? { id, data: row } : null;
    },
    async list() {
      const rows = await fetch('https://cms/posts').then((r) => r.json());
      return rows.map((r) => ({ id: r.slug, data: r }));
    },
    async ids() {
      const rows = await fetch('https://cms/posts?fields=slug').then((r) => r.json());
      return rows.map((r) => r.slug);
    }
  }
});
```

`get(id)` fetches one post on demand — no full list. Mint the wire remotes in a `.remote.ts`:

```ts
import { withRemotes } from 'ogygia/content/server';
export const list = withRemotes(press).list({ map: (e) => ({ id: e.id, title: e.data.title }) });
```

A fetching source defaults to `query` mode — resolved at request time, not build.

## Live stream (push / SSE / subscribe)

Add `live()` — a change **signal**. Yield whenever your feed changes and the collection re-reads. Pair it with `live.list` / `live.get`, which re-emit mapped snapshots on every change:

```ts
let posts = [];

const press = content({
  schema,
  loader: {
    async get(id) { return posts.find((p) => p.id === id) ?? null; },
    async list() { return posts; },
    async ids() { return posts.map((p) => p.id); },
    async *live() {
      for await (const next of subscribeToCms()) { posts = next; yield 1; }
    }
  }
});

export const headlines = withRemotes(press).live.list({ map: (e) => ({ id: e.id, title: e.data.title }) });
```

Yield anything to re-read the whole source, or a **`string[]` of changed ids** for an incremental reload (`get(id)` each) — for a large collection where a full re-list is wasteful.

## Watch it push

Below, a `query.live` yields a rendered [held region](/docs/data-state/held-regions) every second. The server renders each tick and pushes the HTML down the channel; the client **morphs** it in place — no client data code, no per-tick fetch.

<script>
  import LivePartialDemo from '$lib/demos/LivePartialDemo.svelte' with { wake: 'load' };
</script>

<div class="doc-demo">
  <LivePartialDemo />
</div>

## Bodies over the wire

These sources deliver **data** — a CMS row has no compiled `.svx` body. When a client needs to *render* an entry (a live article, a feed card), map it to a [held region](/docs/data-state/held-regions) of one of your own `with { region }` components: `map: (e) => region(Card, e.data)`. `<Region>` fetches and hydrates it — the one renderable that crosses the wire.

> Live sources need a long-lived server. On short-lived serverless functions an in-memory stream is per-isolate — document it, and degrade gracefully rather than promising continuity.
